**Definition 2.2 (initialized lawful image and raw exits).** Let $\Omega$
be all finite actual prefixes at or after $a(u)$ of $P$ with valid original
preparation, canonical variable frames and canonical code supply. This
includes never-install histories, any finite old source-frame prefix before
Install, every prefix of installation, and every prefix of later canonical
source/service executions. Histories start at the original irreducible
primitive entrance, so boot, private preparation and acquisition remain in
their chronology and bill. The four-view assertions begin at $a(u)$.

The image contains unfinished headers, unary lengths and payloads; occupied
input latches; parsing, generation and private gates; source-invalid
overwrite bytes; accepted and rejected candidates; counter preparation,
commitment, unfinished fields and rows; partial and inactive code; banks,
scratch and descriptors; manifest/import cuts; archive records,
publication, both copy stages, final seals, acknowledgement and return.
Erased cells and dead versions are retained. It contains the cut on the
manifest sentinel and each of the two actual entrance moves past L and F.
There is no restriction to word boundaries, synchronized endpoints or
already callable busy cuts.

At an enabled IN both physical bits execute. A bit violating canonical
frame extendibility, or the next required fixed boot/code word, has its
actual latch/control/receipt successor outside $\Omega$. Denote these
one-edge raw exits by $\partial_{\rm raw}\Omega$. They are represented
by the storage construction. No arbitrary later malformed continuation is
defined here. A complete malformed source/service payload retains RCA's
declared raw exit/outcome where specified; it is not made a canonical
source call. Physically disabled, unauthorized and premature unsealed
operations have no execution and no receipt. They are different from raw
executed exits.

